    "explanation": "To the rider: the driver's own GPS trace records 21.2 km against a booked optimal route of 18.4 km — an excess of 2.8 km, or 15.2%, below the 20% (3.68 km) the route-deviation threshold needs — and the platform's derivation prints 'no deviation'. In an ordinary case that would end the matter; it does not here. Between 18:35:00 and 18:42:08 the trace has a 428-second gap, across which it jumps 6.9 km in a straight line — more than a third of the whole trip, and consistent with the driver's KPE-tunnel signal loss. POL-RD-07 makes that trace impaired for policy purposes, and over the impaired stretch the distance cannot be measured: 21.2 km is the shortest, driver-favourable reading, so the true excess could be larger and the 20% test cannot be decided either way. Where the trace is impaired under POL-RD-07(a)–(b) and no independent source named in POL-RD-07(ii) corroborates the route — and none does: the chat log records no detour admission (the only route words are the driver's 'going KPE', which is the optimal corridor itself), the fare breakdown charges no off-route gantry, and trip_record.actual_distance_km is a copy of the same trace — POL-RD-07(iii) and POL-RD-10(3) require ESCALATE rather than a dismissal. Your case is not rejected; it goes to a human reviewer, and the disputed $34.42 is held, not collected or refunded.\n\nTo the driver: nothing here finds you detoured. Your 'going KPE' is the optimal corridor, the $3.00 of ERP lies on the optimal route with no off-route gantry billed, and no traffic advisory exists either to exonerate or to implicate you (EV-ADVIS-00110-01 is empty, weight 0.0). This escalation is a data failure, not a finding against you.\n\nFigures used: trace_distance_km 21.2, optimal_distance_km 18.4, excess 2.8 km / 15.2%, charged_total $34.42, recomputed optimal-route fare $25.68, and — only if a deviation were later established — a POL-RD-02 refund of $8.75; that fallback is not this ruling. No referral fires: POL-RD